,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Can rely on Rule 6c-11 (subject to its conditions):**\n- Open-end ETFs, both index-based and fully transparent active. Conditions include daily portfolio holdings disclosure, written basket policies and website disclosures.\n- Leveraged/inverse ETFs. The 2020 Rule 18f-4 release removed their exclusion and rescinded their orders, so they must comply with 18f-4. That means a derivatives risk management program and the relative VaR limit (200% of a reference portfolio), which effectively caps new funds at about 2x. Pre-October 2020 funds above 2x were grandfathered. The SEC staff's late-2025 letters reaffirmed this cap against 3x–5x filings.\n\n**Need separate exemptive relief:**\n- UIT ETFs.\n- Non-transparent or semi-transparent active ETFs, which need their own orders.\n- ETF share classes of multi-class funds. Dimensional's 2025 order was the first, followed by others.\n\n**Outside the 1940 Act entirely:**\n- Commodity and crypto trusts are registered under the '33 Act and list under exchange rules, including the 2025 generic standards.\n\nMy knowledge runs to June 2026, so please verify any later changes.","ttft_ms":10477,"type":"result","duration_ms":14450,"uuid":"9c6be904-a02e-43d7-a7f1-c1d261522954","ttft_stream_ms":1392,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":1392,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
